-- ============================================================================
-- payer-rules-sc-carecoord-absent.sql
--
-- Eleven care-management codes that the South Carolina Medicaid physician fee
-- schedule DOES NOT LIST. Before this, all five SC plans answered nothing at
-- all for them: a biller looking up principal care management, transitional
-- care management or home-health certification for an SC Medicaid member got
-- silence, which is indistinguishable from "we never checked".
--
-- WHY coverage_status = 'unknown' AND NOT 'not_covered'
-- The schedule contains explicit priced rows for the codes on either side of
-- each of these. It does not contain a "not covered" designation for the codes
-- themselves — it contains NOTHING for them. An absent row is not a refusal to
-- pay, and storing it as one would invent a payer decision that no document
-- makes. 'unknown' also keeps these out of the code picker by default, which
-- is right: nothing here establishes them as billable.
--
-- HOW EACH ABSENCE WAS PROVED
-- Not by a failed search — by an adjacent-row bracket. For every code the two
-- rows that WOULD surround it were located and quoted, showing the schedule
-- runs straight past. 99424-99427 sit inside a schedule that jumps 99418 ->
-- 99439; 99491 inside 99490 -> 99492; 99495/99496 inside 99494 -> 99497;
-- G0179-G0181 inside G0166 -> G0237; G0318 inside G0239 -> G0372. A zero-hit
-- search can mean "absent" or "my query was wrong"; a populated bracket can
-- only mean absent.
--
-- SCOPE. These are SC Medicaid managed care plans, and the cited schedule is
-- SC Medicaid fee-for-service. That inference is the library's existing,
-- deliberate practice for SC (see payer-rules-sc-fee-schedule.sql, 110 rules
-- on this same document) and the answers say plainly which document they come
-- from, so a biller can see the derivation rather than take it on trust.
--
-- Each quote is a row citation in the established fee-schedule form, which
-- scripts/recheck-source-drift.mjs verifies field by field.
-- ============================================================================

BEGIN;

-- Nothing currently answers these keys; this keeps a later seed from
-- leaving two live rows on one key.
UPDATE payer_rule pr
   SET expiration_date = GREATEST(pr.effective_date, DATE '2026-01-01')
 WHERE pr.expiration_date IS NULL
   AND pr.created_by <> 'extract:sc-carecoord-absent-2026-08'
   AND (pr.payer_id, pr.state, pr.code, pr.attribute, pr.product_line) IN (
